;;; ccr-ch3c-pb.scm -- CCR.pdf chapter 3, Lemma 3.4 in the commutant form:
;;;
;;;     T irreducible on K^n, X T = T X and |det X|_K <= 1   imply   X is power bounded,
;;;
;;; over a LOCAL NV field K, and the notes' form (X = T) as a corollary (ccr-3-4-comm, ccr-3-4); then the
;;; ultrametric half of Proposition 3.6 on the commutant of T (ccr-3-6-ultra-inv, ccr-3-6-ultra: S, R
;;; commuting with T, each invertible when its determinant is non-zero, give |det(S + R)| <= max(|det S|,
;;; |det R|); the tree has no adjugate, so the invertibility is a hypothesis).  Agent CCR-1109-B3, wave
;;; 2026-11-09.  Destination: theorem-library/ccr-ch3c-pb.scm.  Library: analysis.  Helper prefix cd8-.
;;;
;;; ROUTE (the notes' proof, with B commuting with T instead of with X; CCR-1109-B2 s.4.1).
;;; Suppose X is not power bounded.  For every k there are j and an entry of X^j of norm > k + 1; take
;;; beta, an entry of X^j of MAXIMAL norm (cd8-mat-argmax, a finite argmax read through the Cantor codes),
;;; and Y_k = beta^(-1) X^j.  Then ||Y_k||_max = 1 with an entry of norm 1, Y_k T = T Y_k, and
;;; |det Y_k| (k + 1) <= 1, because |det Y_k| = |det(beta^(-1) I)| |det X^j| <= |beta|^(-n) <= |beta|^(-1).
;;; The Y_k are chosen as one sequence (rmm-nn-choice); the matrix ball is sequentially compact
;;; (ccr-mat-ball-seq-compact), so a subsequence converges entrywise to some B with ||B|| <= 1.  The limit
;;; commutes with T (the products are close: cd8-close-mul-right/-left), has det B = 0 (ccr-det-close and
;;; the decay of det Y_k), so B = 0 (ccr-3-4-commutant); but the Y_k have an entry of norm 1.
;;;
;;; LOAD WINDOW: after theorem-library/ccr-ch3c-ball (ccr-mat-ball-seq-compact, cd7-code-bound) and
;;; ccr-ch3c-det (ccr-det-close), this agent; after ccr-ch3-ops (CCR-1109-B: cb3-*, ccr-3-1-eq21,
;;; ccr-3-6-multiplicative) and ccr-ch3b-alg (CCR-1109-B2: ccr-3-4-commutant); all unwired.

